1)Conditional rendering — *ngIf vs @if (For Condtional Rendering Insted of ngif we can use @if)
Old syntax:
<div *ngIf="isLoggedIn">
  Welcome back!
</div>

New syntax:
@if (isLoggedIn) {
  <div>Welcome back!</div>
} @else {
  <div>Please log in.</div>
}

2. Looping — *ngFor vs @for

Old syntax:
<div *ngFor="let product of products; let i = index">
  {{ i + 1 }}. {{ product.name }}
</div>


New syntax:
@for (product of products; track product.id; let i = $index) {
  <div>{{ i + 1 }}. {{ product.name }}</div>
} @empty {
  <p>No products found.</p>
}


Know it:
- @for is used to iterate over a collection.
- track product.id helps Angular identify each item and efficiently update the DOM.
- $index gives the current item's index.
- @empty displays content when the collection is empty.

old way//
<div *ngFor="let product of products; trackBy: trackById">
  {{ product.name }}
</div>
trackById(index: number, product: Product) {
  return product.id;
}


// new way
@for (product of products; track product.id) {
  <div>
    {{ product.name }}
  </div>
